<!-- ══ BIG STAT ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="stat-sec wrap">
  <p class="eyebrow rv">In AMPLIFY's national survey of 2,535 Australians</p>
  <p class="giant grad"><span class="count" data-target="73">73</span>%</p>
  <p class="stat-cap rv">sit in a <em>conditional middle</em>. Neither fixed supporters nor fixed opponents — their views on density depend on <em>how</em>, <em>where</em>, and <em>under what conditions</em> new housing gets built.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══ METHOD ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="sec wrap" id="method">
  <p class="eyebrow rv">The methodology</p>
  <h2 class="rv">A new way to find agreement where headlines see only fight.</h2>
  <p class="intro rv">Most consultations ask what people <strong>oppose</strong>. We built a process that asks a different question: once we understand each other's positions, what might we move forward on <strong>together</strong>?</p>

  <div class="publics">
    <div class="pub a rv">
      <div class="tag">Stage 1 · Sessions 1–2</div>
      <div class="n"><span class="count" data-target="22">22</span></div>
      <h4>The engaged public</h4>
      <p>Highly informed, actively involved residents — both opponents and supporters of density, drawn from eight Sydney LGAs. The people who show up to fight over developments. They mapped the arguments first.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pub b rv">
      <div class="tag">Stage 2 · Sessions 3–4</div>
      <div class="n"><span class="count" data-target="150">150</span></div>
      <h4>The representative public</h4>
      <p>Recruited to reflect the same neighbourhoods — no particular stake in housing. They tested whether the engaged public's bridging conditions held with everyday Sydneysiders. 58 returned for a fourth session.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="tools rv">
    <div class="tool">
      <div class="step">01 →</div>
      <h5>Pol.is</h5>
      <p class="role">Mapping the landscape</p>
      <p>An open-source platform that clusters opinion, and then surfaces the statements that cross the divide it finds. Popularity and bridging are different measures, and it reports the second one.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tool">
      <div class="step">02 →</div>
      <h5>Quadratic voting</h5>
      <p class="role">Pricing intensity</p>
      <p>Participants spend a limited budget of voice credits across priorities; each extra vote on one item costs quadratically more. Forces genuine trade-offs, revealing what people actually value most.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tool">
      <div class="step">03 →</div>
      <h5>Stanford Online Deliberation</h5>
      <p class="role">Testing against lived experience</p>
      <p>Structured small-group video conversations with an automated moderator. Turns aggregate signals into human specifics — clarifying ambiguity, surfacing local context, letting quieter voices speak.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tool">
      <div class="step">04 →</div>
      <h5>Baseline → endline</h5>
      <p class="role">Measuring movement</p>
      <p>Matched surveys before and after each cohort's process, to see whether structured deliberation shifts positions, perceptions of the other side, or both.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ FINDINGS ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="sec wrap" id="findings">
  <p class="eyebrow rv">Three findings</p>
  <h2 class="rv">What we learned when we actually asked.</h2>
  <p class="intro rv">Two publics. Four sessions. 392 statements, 24,913 votes. Here's what emerged.</p>

  <div class="finds">
    <div class="find rv">
      <div class="num">01</div>
      <div>
        <h3>The loudest objections turned out to be negotiable.</h3>
        <p>Nine in ten of the group most strongly opposed to density would accept a development they dislike — if their community had real power over what gets built. The representative public ratified the same trade across its own divide. Community power is, before anything else, the condition of those who would otherwise say no.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="find rv">
      <div class="num">02</div>
      <div>
        <h3>The people who fight hardest agree on a long list of specifics.</h3>
        <p>Public transport, open space protected as density rises, homes essential workers can afford, a greater mix of housing types, design quality, schools and clinics attended to early, affordability defined at 30% of income. Several of these carried <em>every</em> participant on one side and strong majorities on the other. What divides them is much shorter: how high to build, whether supply alone delivers affordability, and whether infrastructure comes before housing or with it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="find rv">
      <div class="num">03</div>
      <div>
        <h3>A representative public arrived, on its own, at the same priorities.</h3>
        <p>150 Sydneysiders with no particular stake in housing, recruited from the same neighbourhoods. Their quadratic vote put public transport first and green space second, both inside the top three the engaged cohort had set. They arrived there having never seen the engaged public's ballot.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ CONDITIONS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="sec wrap" id="conditions">
  <p class="eyebrow rv">Six conditions</p>
  <h2 class="rv">Density done well, in Sydneysiders' own words.</h2>
  <p class="intro rv">The bridging conditions that surfaced across every instrument the program used.</p>

  <div class="conds">
    <div class="cond rv">
      <p class="label">01 · Transport</p>
      <h4>Trains and buses that actually turn up.</h4>
      <p class="fig grad"><span class="count" data-target="93">93</span>%</p>
      <p class="desc">of the representative public agree better public transport and local amenities are the condition on density. It topped the quadratic vote in <em>both</em> publics.</p>
      <p class="quote">“It just needs to be continually flowing so that the moment I step out, I have a choice.”</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cond rv">
      <p class="label">02 · Green space</p>
      <h4>Parks are not a nice-to-have.</h4>
      <p class="fig grad"><span class="count" data-target="94">94</span>%</p>
      <p class="desc">of height sceptics — and 91% of supporters — agree that as density rises, guaranteed parks and open space matter more with every extra storey.</p>
      <p class="quote">“Centuries and centuries old.” A park, once the land is taken, does not come back.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cond rv">
      <p class="label">03 · Accountability</p>
      <h4>Developers held to what they build.</h4>
      <p class="fig grad"><span class="count" data-target="99">99</span>%</p>
      <p class="desc">the highest-agreement statement in the entire program. Nobody asked about developers — it came up anyway, in room after room.</p>
      <p class="quote">“They claim bankruptcy and just disappear.” The builder's mistake becomes the owner's bill.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cond rv">
      <p class="label">04 · Affordability</p>
      <h4>Pegged to income. Permanent. Enforced.</h4>
      <p class="fig grad"><span class="count" data-target="30">30</span>%</p>
      <p class="desc">of household income — the definition three of four engaged groups arrived at independently, set against the discount-off-market definition in current schemes.</p>
      <p class="quote">Both camps agree who it is for: essential workers, young people, first-home buyers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cond rv">
      <p class="label">05 · Built form</p>
      <h4>Height that steps down.</h4>
      <p class="fig grad sm">71% <span class="slash">/</span> 68%</p>
      <p class="desc">of height sceptics and supporters both accept “high-rises on main roads, low-rise on backstreets”. One of the few built-form statements to carry the divide.</p>
      <p class="quote">Concentrated growth near transport hubs is the shape of density both sides can name.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cond rv">
      <p class="label">06 · Consultation</p>
      <h4>Early, strategic, and representative.</h4>
      <p class="fig grad sm">80% <span class="slash">/</span> 81%</p>
      <p class="desc">want communities setting vision and rules through long-term plans, rather than litigating every proposal one at a time. Both camps, near-identical numbers.</p>
      <p class="quote">“A tick box exercise.” Consultation arrives after the decisions have been made.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ PULL QUOTE ════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="pull wrap">
  <blockquote class="rv">I think in general people aren't opposed to development itself, they are opposed to development that is done without appropriate planning and infrastructure to go alongside it.</blockquote>
  <cite class="rv">Representative public participant · endline survey</cite>
</section>

<!-- ══ THE DIVIDE ════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="sec wrap" id="divide">
  <p class="eyebrow rv">The one real divide</p>
  <h2 class="rv">Height splits the room. Until you give it a reason.</h2>
  <p class="intro rv">Asked to reject high-rise outright, <strong>75%</strong> of the sceptic cluster agreed (R-s45). Every statement below runs the other way — agreeing means <em>accepting</em> high-rise. Watch how far the sceptics travel as the condition attached to it gets more concrete.</p>

  <div class="legend rv">
    <p class="lt">How to read the bars</p>
    <p><span class="swatch sw-n"></span><strong>Red</strong> grows from the left: agreement among height sceptics, the NIMBY-leaning cluster (108 people). <span class="swatch sw-y"></span><strong>Blue</strong> grows from the right: agreement among high-rise supporters, the YIMBY-leaning cluster (40 people).</p>
    <p><span class="swatch sw-f"></span>Where the two overlap they add together and <strong>ignite into pale rose-lilac</strong>. A wide bright band means the statement carried both camps — agreement across difference, as literal additive light. Two separated stubs with black between them means a divide.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="play rv">
    <p class="hint">↓ The statement being voted on · agreeing means accepting high-rise</p>
    <p id="stmt">NIMBYs/Boomers who complain about high rises need to accept we are in a growing city.</p>

    <div class="bigmix" id="bigmix">
      <div class="f n" id="bf-n"></div>
      <div class="f y" id="bf-y"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="bigmix-labels">
      <div class="a"><span id="bv-n">41%</span><span class="sub">Height sceptics · 108</span></div>
      <div class="b"><span id="bv-y">87%</span><span class="sub">High-rise supporters · 40</span></div>
    </div>

    <p class="hint" style="margin-top:2.4rem">↓ Attach a condition and watch the sceptic bar climb</p>
    <div class="toggles" id="toggles">
      <button class="tg on" data-key="bare">No condition — just build up</button>
      <button class="tg" data-key="opportunity">…it creates places to live</button>
      <button class="tg" data-key="land">…the city has no land left</button>
      <button class="tg" data-key="form">…on main roads, low-rise behind</button>
      <button class="tg" data-key="stations">…near existing stations</button>
      <button class="tg" data-key="affordable">…with affordable housing attached</button>
    </div>

    <p class="play-note">What the sceptics reject is <em>blanket</em> verticality, and they will say so at 75%. Asserted bare, high-rise still carries 41% of them. Tied to a place, a station, or a promise about affordability, it carries 81% — and the bar goes white all the way across.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="rows">
    <div class="row"><div class="row-top"><div class="row-id">R-s45</div><p class="row-txt">No to massive high-rise buildings</p></div>
      <div class="mix" style="--a:75;--b:3"><div class="f n"></div><div class="f y"></div></div>
      <div class="row-nums"><span class="a">75%</span><span class="b">3%</span></div></div>
    <div class="row"><div class="row-top"><div class="row-id">R-s125</div><p class="row-txt">Sydney lacks the space to build horizontally, so vertical development may be the way</p></div>
      <div class="mix" style="--a:51;--b:90"><div class="f n"></div><div class="f y"></div></div>
      <div class="row-nums"><span class="a">51%</span><span class="b">90%</span></div></div>
    <div class="row"><div class="row-top"><div class="row-id">R-s175</div><p class="row-txt">High-rises on main roads, low-rise on backstreets — density done right</p></div>
      <div class="mix" style="--a:71;--b:68"><div class="f n"></div><div class="f y"></div></div>
      <div class="row-nums"><span class="a">71%</span><span class="b">68%</span></div></div>
    <div class="row"><div class="row-top"><div class="row-id">R-s86</div><p class="row-txt">High density with shops and amenities near existing stations</p></div>
      <div class="mix" style="--a:72;--b:81"><div class="f n"></div><div class="f y"></div></div>
      <div class="row-nums"><span class="a">72%</span><span class="b">81%</span></div></div>
    <div class="row"><div class="row-top"><div class="row-id">R-s191</div><p class="row-txt">More affordable housing; high-rises are fine in certain areas</p></div>
      <div class="mix" style="--a:81;--b:90"><div class="f n"></div><div class="f y"></div></div>
      <div class="row-nums"><span class="a">81%</span><span class="b">90%</span></div></div>
    <div class="row"><div class="row-top"><div class="row-id">R-s168</div><p class="row-txt">Developers need to be held accountable for dodgy building standards</p></div>
      <div class="mix" style="--a:98;--b:100"><div class="f n"></div><div class="f y"></div></div>
      <div class="row-nums"><span class="a">98%</span><span class="b">100%</span></div></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ MOVEMENT ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="sec wrap" id="movement">
  <p class="eyebrow rv">Before → after</p>
  <h2 class="rv">Did deliberation move people?</h2>
  <p class="intro rv">Positions themselves barely shifted. What moved was how each side saw the other — and what their opposition was actually about.</p>

  <div class="moves">
    <div class="move rv">
      <p class="big grad"><span class="count" data-target="43">43</span><span class="of">/46</span></p>
      <h4>found more common ground than they expected.</h4>
      <p>93% of the representative public, and none disagreed. The same share felt able to genuinely express their views; 41 of 46 agreed a process like this would be a good way to shape housing policy.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="move rv">
      <p class="big grad sm">−0.33 → +0.27</p>
      <h4>Opposition moved off procedure and onto delivery.</h4>
      <p>Among the engaged public, the strongest disqualifier at baseline was procedural — density built with residents having little say. After deliberation it flipped to net acceptance, the largest shift of any condition tested. Meanwhile the delivery conditions <em>hardened</em>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="move rv">
      <p class="big grad sm">Every item</p>
      <h4>of perception rose in the engaged public.</h4>
      <p>Feeling understood started as the survey's lowest item and was the only one to move up for some and down for none. Understanding of others rose alongside it, as did the sense that the other side is open to compromise.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

